WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.536
The agent loop at a glance.

2
00:00:01.816 --> 00:00:05.379
Strip away the frameworks and every agent runs the same loop.

3
00:00:05.659 --> 00:00:10.245
The Hugging Face Agents Course calls it the Thought, Action, Observation cycle.

4
00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:19.997
The model reasons about what to do (thought), emits a tool call with arguments (action), and your code runs the tool and feeds the result back (observation).

5
00:00:20.277 --> 00:00:26.101
The model reads the observation and either takes another action or produces a final answer.

6
00:00:26.381 --> 00:00:32.013
The course compares it to a while loop whose rules live in the system prompt and tool definitions.

7
00:00:32.293 --> 00:00:34.213
The pattern has a research lineage.

8
00:00:34.493 --> 00:00:52.221
ReAct (Yao and colleagues, 2022) showed that interleaving reasoning traces with actions helped models on question answering and interactive tasks, partly because actions let the model look things up instead of hallucinating, and the reasoning helped it track and update a plan.

9
00:00:52.501 --> 00:01:05.024
Today’s APIs build this in: in Studio 1 you saw that a Claude response with stop_reason equal to tool_use is the model asking your code to act, and the SDK’s tool runner automates the loop.

10
00:01:05.304 --> 00:01:08.739
What the model cannot do is stop the loop safely on its own.

11
00:01:09.019 --> 00:01:11.557
It may decide it is finished too early, or never.

12
00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:22.205
So every loop you write has at least two exits: the model’s final answer, and a budget your code enforces (a maximum number of steps, tokens, dollars, or seconds).

13
00:01:22.485 --> 00:01:32.320
Real loops add more: a stop when a write needs human approval, a stop when the same tool call repeats with the same arguments, and an error path when a tool fails.

14
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.437
Week 2 is entirely about building this loop well.

15
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:42.352
For now, notice its shape, and notice where your code sits: between every action the model requests and the world.
